WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.280
 Packet switching was an idea which was specifically studied by Len Kleinrock at MIT who was actually

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.900
 looking at message switching and he did a brilliant dissertation on the use of queuing

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:24.700
 theory to analyze what networks of queues would look like using this message switching

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:32.620
 approach. His analysis, although we'd never used the word packet, is as equally applicable

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:36.820
 to packet switching as it is to message switching. So that's one important milestone in around

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:43.820
 1961. In '62 or thereabouts, Paul Barron is doing work for the RAND Corporation and

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:50.740
 is deeply concerned about the ability to preserve command and control in a post-nuclear environment.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:55.180
 We were seriously worried that the Russians would actually launch and that we would suffer

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:58.940
 a nuclear attack and that we had to be able to respond and we needed command and control

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:08.900
 for that. So Paul, in 1962, before the existence of integrated circuits or anything else, is

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:18.140
 saying we really should be digitizing and packetizing voice and then using sort of pole-mounted

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:24.420
 radios that are able to transmit in all directions to create a highly connected environment so

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:29.720
 that if holes were knocked out of it by nuclear explosions, if it's a fabric that's in any

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:34.980
 way connected, information could get from one end to the other. So he envisioned the

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:40.420
 notion of a message block and it was dynamically routed. He used hot potato routing. If you

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:44.260
 got something, you got rid of it as fast as you could. He chopped up the speech into little

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:49.340
 20 millisecond pieces. He didn't talk so much about data as I remember it. This was

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:54.160
 supposed to be a highly resilient voice network for command and control. I may have just done

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:00.780
 him a disservice because later he was very much conscious of the importance of data communication

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:07.440
 too. So that's around 1962. It gets documented in an 11-volume series called On Distributed

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:14.240
 Communications and he can't sell it to anybody. The traditional telcos, AT&T in particular,

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:20.420
 found the people at what was then the Defense Communications Agency, laughed him out of

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:26.040
 the room, said this was a silly idea, couldn't possibly work, and so he should just go away.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:32.220
 So he never got anywhere with that in spite of all the documentation. In '66, Larry Roberts,

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:37.380
 along with one other guy whose name I am now not remembering, does a point-to-point experiment

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:45.260
 to test packet switching. It was between the ANSF-Q7 machine at System Development Corporation

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:52.260
 in Santa Monica and the TX-2 machine at MIT at Lincoln Laboratory, which is where Larry

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:58.140
 was. They demonstrated on a 2400-bit line, bit-per-second line, that you could move packets

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:05.380
 back and forth. Then in the '64 or '65 timeframe, a man named Donald W. Davies at the National

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:12.180
 Physical Laboratory in London also gets the bug, tries to get money from the Science Research

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:20.460
 Commission in England, and gets only enough to build one node, the one-node network. So

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:25.140
 he builds this packet net. He invents the term "packet" to describe what these objects

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:30.460
 are and it works. He's got a bunch of terminals and other things hanging off of this one node.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:36.940
 So in a funny way, he built a local area network, if you like, but it was based on physical

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:42.660
 wires. So those three guys introduced packet switching. Larry and whoever it was that he

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:47.780
 worked with demonstrate that it's possible to get two very distinct kinds of computers

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:54.740
 to talk to each other using the standard way. J.C.R. Licklider is a psychologist, actually,

00:03:54.740 --> 00:04:01.420
 at MIT, but he's convinced in the early '60s that computing is important to non-numeric

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:06.140
 processing, that it will allow people to work together and collaborate in ways that they

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:10.900
 never could before. He comes and starts the Information Processing Techniques Office at

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:18.500
 ARPA with this bee in his bonnet. And who does he encounter? He encounters Douglas Engelbart

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:24.300
 at SRI International, and the two bond, basically, because Engelbart was all about non-numeric

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:29.700
 computing and the ability of people to build up the superstructure of communications and

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:35.940
 documents and interact with each other, hyperlinking, the mouse, the portrait mode display, black

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:43.860
 on white presentations. I mean, the guy had a worldwide web in a box at SRI, and Licklider

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:49.300
 understands that. Licklider is sending out notes to his little community of people talking

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:56.740
 about the intergalactic network made of tongue-in-cheek. So he really gets credit for having put this

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:03.060
 meme in place at ARPA. Then Taylor comes along to pick up the responsibility for running

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.940
 the IPTO from Licklider and is all hacked off because he's got three terminals in his

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:12.020
 office at the Pentagon connected to three different machines, and he says, "Why can't

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:18.060
 there be one terminal talking to all three? We need a network." And so as he's pursuing

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:23.820
 this idea with Charlie Hertzfeld, who was the head of ARPA at the time, Charlie hands

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:28.660
 him a million bucks over a 20-minute conversation, and now Taylor's got the problem. Who's going

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:33.460
 to actually do this? Because Taylor's not a technologist either. He's another kind of

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:39.460
 psychologist type. So he decides to get Larry Roberts from Lincoln Laboratories, who did

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:44.580
 that packet thing, and Larry doesn't want to come. So he goes and complains about this

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:49.260
 to Charlie Hertzfeld. Charlie calls up the guy that runs Lincoln Laboratories and says,

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:54.820
 "You know, we pay for a significant fraction of your research budget every year. You should

00:05:54.820 --> 00:06:00.540
 tell Larry he should show up." In fact, I thought that maybe Larry had been forced to

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:06.100
 do this by Charlie. I think it was probably a little less awful than that. But Larry was

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:10.340
 persuaded to come down. Eventually, of course, inherited the operation of the office from

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:17.780
 Bob Taylor, but in the meantime is the guy responsible for initiating the ARPANET project.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:25.180
 So they write an RFQ request for quotation. A bunch of responses come back, probably on

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:29.300
 the order of a couple dozen. I don't know personally for sure how many. I know that

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:36.320
 I wrote one of them with my colleague Steve Crocker while we were still at UCLA as graduate

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:41.460
 students, but we were consulting with a company called Jacoby Systems in Santa Monica. Jacoby

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:46.020
 Systems wrote one of the responses. Bolt-Bear-Neck and Newman wrote another response, primarily

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:52.580
 written by Bob Cohn, who'd come to BB&N from MIT. So the responses come back and they get

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:58.060
 evaluated and four of them end up, and the Jacoby Systems one isn't one of them. Or if

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:02.980
 it was, it didn't get selected. BB&N got selected. So Steve Crocker and I kind of hiked back

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:08.940
 to UCLA as graduate students. And the next thing we know, Len Kleinrock, who's at UCLA

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:13.580
 and who'd written this original dissertation work on packet switching, has come to UCLA

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:22.180
 to teach and explore a queuing theory, is a close compatriot of Larry Roberts, because

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:26.100
 they were both at Lincoln MIOBS together. So he gets the network measurement center

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:32.020
 piece of the ARPANET project. And Steve Crocker and I and John Postel, all of us from the

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:40.340
 same high school in San Fernando Valley, end up in Len Kleinrock's operation running the

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:44.820
 network measurement center. So I was the principal programmer for that. Steve Crocker took the

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:50.020
 responsibility for managing and leading the network working group, which led to the host

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:55.300
 to host protocols. And John Postel eventually becomes the keeper of the documentation. He's

00:07:55.300 --> 00:08:00.600
 the RFC editor, which Steve Crocker started, Request for Comments. He's the guy that becomes

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:05.480
 the numbers czar, which is keeping track of address spaces and allocations, and eventually

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:10.420
 becomes the domain name manager of the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority when the Internet

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:17.300
 happens. That hasn't happened yet. This period of time of the ARPANET program brings us up

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:22.740
 to 1972. This is an important moment in this whole history, because the first demonstration

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:29.420
 of the ARPANET happens in the Washington Hilton Hotel basement in October of '72. A whole

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:35.260
 bunch of people from the networking, interested networking community, packet switching community,

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:40.660
 attend, not only in the U.S., but from France and from England and Italy and Germany and

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:48.900
 elsewhere. That group of about 25 or 30 people convenes, sees the ARPANET in operation, sees

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:55.580
 applications that were being done, including Doug Engelbart's stuff, and then forms this

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:59.620
 international network working group modeled after the working group that Steve Crocker

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:05.260
 managed to build the ARPANET system. And at this point, I become the chairman of that

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:11.940
 group because Steve is busy at ARPA doing artificial intelligence.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:17.860
 At the end of that year, Bob Kahn leaves Bolt, Berenick, and Newman and goes to ARPA. I leave

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:23.660
 UCLA, where I'd been working with Kleinrock and Crocker and Postel, and they go to Stanford.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:30.940
 So Bob is at ARPA. I'm at Stanford. And in the spring of '73, Bob comes out from ARPA

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:33.660
 and he says, "I have a problem." He says, "What's your problem?" He says, "Well, we

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:40.120
 got this ARPANET." He says, "Yep. But we also are working on other networking capability

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:44.420
 to make command and control work for the military. If you're going to be serious about putting

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:49.960
 computers in command and control, they have to be mobile. They have to be in armored personnel

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:54.460
 vehicles and tanks and all these other things. They have to be seaborne so we can have ship-to-ship

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:59.500
 and ship-to-shore communications, which means satellite. So we need mobile radio. We need

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:06.140
 satellite in addition to the fixed-wire systems that are represented by the ARPANET. We have

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:10.420
 fixed installations that are not moving around." So we have all these different technologies.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:16.300
 And Bob's brilliant idea is not to build one network with all those technologies embedded

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:21.340
 in it. Instead, he breaks them apart and says, "Let's build a packet satellite network,

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:25.360
 which optimizes the use of satellite, takes into account that it's got a half a second

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:31.420
 round trip time. Let's build a packet radio network, which optimizes a system whose connectivity

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:36.580
 is changing with time as things move around and as you get variable delay and also variable

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:41.300
 interference." So these were three different packet networks. Then the problem is how do

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:45.260
 you hook them together? You wouldn't have this problem if you put all the technologies

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:49.660
 into one net. But if you put them all into one net, it makes it really hard to do control

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:54.260
 over all these highly variable parameters. So instead, he says, "Break them into different

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:59.100
 networks and connect those together." So we design and build a gateway, which today we

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:04.780
 call a router. And that concept also introduced a whole bunch of other things like how do

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:09.680
 you refer to another network? Each network thinks it's the only network in the universe.

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:15.420
 This is true of the proprietary networks like SNA and DECnet and so on. You didn't have

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:20.460
 a vocabulary that said take this packet and move it to another computer on another network

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:25.020
 somewhere else that you might not even be connected to. So we have to invent an internet

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:31.780
 address space in order to solve that problem. We have to find a way to allow packet losses

00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:37.900
 in this path to be recovered, which is where TCP now becomes a manager of reliability on

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:42.820
 an end-to-end basis instead of relying on each net to be reliable. The ARPAnet was built

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:46.820
 on the assumption you could build a reliable underlying net. The internet was based on

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:51.260
 the assumption that no network was necessarily reliable and you had to do end-to-end retransmissions

00:11:51.260 --> 00:12:00.900
 to recover. So during this 1973 period, Bob and I get the first paper written and published

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:07.620
 in IEEE Transactions on Communications, May of 1974. And I think mostly nobody paid too

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:13.500
 much attention to it. Meanwhile, ARPA is funding us to go make this actually work. At Stanford,

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:20.580
 I am working with my graduate students, some who are at Xerox PARC, some who are at Stanford,

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:25.740
 on the detailed specifications of TCP/IP. We published that in December of 1974 and

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:30.260
 it's the first time the word "internet" shows up in print anywhere. It was the first

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:35.940
 papers we talked about internetworking. So internet shows up in a complete spec in December

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:40.220
 of 1974 and it's also got bugs, but we don't know that yet. Until we start implementing

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:45.940
 it in 1975 with two other organizations. So Bob Kahn says, "You can't have just one

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:49.900
 implementation." Bolt-Bear-Neck and Newman becomes one of the implementers. University

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:55.420
 College London and Peter Kristine's group in England is the second or third implementation.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:13:00.260
 So we have three implementations of TCP/IP. In fact, by this time it's only TCP. We haven't

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:06.100
 broken off the IP part. Three implementations are going. We instantly find problems with

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:12.660
 the design and we start evolving. So over a period from 1973 to 1978 we go through four

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:18.660
 iterations of the design and implementation and test until we have a fairly stable thing.

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:24.580
 And then we standardize. So 1978 we fix everything. Now by this time the internet protocol has

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:31.340
 been split off because people like David Reed and Danny Cohen are saying we need to have

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:36.340
 real-time communications that is not necessarily reliable but which has a little latency. So

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:40.320
 voice communications, radar tracks and all that. You don't care where the missile was

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:45.460
 two seconds ago. You want to know where is it now? And in the case of voice if you lose

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:53.140
 a packet you just say that again. I missed something. So we split TCP into TCP and IP

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:57.140
 and we create something called user datagram protocol which is parallel to TCP and it is

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:04.300
 the real-time low latency version of the reliable TCP. All of those little components, IP, TCP

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:10.600
 and UDP now go into the internet architecture starting in 1978 and we start implementing.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:17.940
 For the next five years we do everything we can to get TCP/IP implemented on every operating

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:23.620
 system we can find. It goes onto the IBM machines, it goes onto the digital machines, HP, it

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:29.020
 goes into Unix. We have a Unix version built by Bolt, Barenik and Newman. We send it out

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:33.940
 to Berkeley, to the Berkeley BSD release guys and Bill Joyce says I don't like that code.

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:41.420
 He writes his own, puts it into BSD 4.2 and that's the version of Unix that carries a

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:46.540
 lot of TCP/IP to the academic world because at the same sort of time frame some micro

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:51.740
 systems comes along and builds these fantastic workstations and they want to use open source

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:58.700
 or at least open protocols and open operating systems. So they adopt Unix and the TCP/IP

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:02.940
 comes with it and they use Ethernet as a way of connecting workstations together so they

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:08.080
 are the engine that's driving the academic community which are all gangbusters for workstations

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:12.500
 and high-speed local networking. So all of this of course places huge demands on the

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:17.860
 ARPANET backbone which is only running at 50 kilobits a second and eventually leads

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:23.020
 to the need for higher speed. NSF jumps into the fray seeing how valuable all this is for

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:27.620
 the academic community and concludes that it should build a network that runs even faster

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:29.540
 and it does so and it's called NSFNET.

